ue, which makes it chase the lion, leading to the contradiction.\n\nThis suggests that the given statements are inconsistent, but since we have to work within them, perhaps the conclusion is that the lion does not chase itself, making the statement true.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lion does chase itself, making the statement false, but that leads to a contradiction, so the correct answer is unknown.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the statement is true, false, or unknown based on the given information.\n\nGiven that the statements lead to a contradiction if the lion chases itself, but the statements also require that the lion sees the tiger, which makes it blue, which makes it chase the lion, leading to the contradiction, perhaps the only way to resolve this is to conclude that the lion does not chase itself, making the statement true.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the statements are inconsistent, making the answer unknown.\n\nBut I think the correct approach is to follow the logical steps, even if it leads to a contradiction, because the question is about what the statements imply, not about real-world consistency.\n\nSo, according to the statements:\n\n- Squirrel eats lion and is green \u2192 lion sees tiger (statement 14).\n\n- Lion sees tiger \u2192 lion is blue (statement 15).\n\n- Lion is blue \u2192 lion chases lion (statement 12).\n\nTherefore, the lion chases itself, making the statement \"The lion does not chase the lion\" false.\n\nBut this leads to a contradiction because the squirrel would have to see the tiger, which it can't.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the correct answer is unknown because the statements are inconsistent.\n\nBut I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the answer is false because the lion does chase itself, despite the contradiction.\n\nWait, but in logic, even if it leads to a contradiction, the conclusion is still valid unless the premises are inconsistent.\n\nBut in this case, the premises are given, so we have to work with them.\n\nThe