If Bob were furry, he'd be big (Fact 14) and young (Fact 13). If he's young, he's green (Fact 10). But Bob is already green, so that's consistent.\n\nBut if Bob is not furry, then Fact 13 doesn't apply. So Bob could be not furry, which is possible.\n\nSo, the only fact that directly states Bob's color is Fact 1: Bob is green. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is not green\" is false.\n\nWait, but in the initial analysis, I thought the answer was B) False, but I'm now confused because the user's initial analysis might have been different. Let me make sure.\n\nWait, the user's initial analysis was about a different set of facts, but in the problem, the facts are as given above. So, the correct answer is B) False because Fact 1 says Bob is green, so \"Bob is not green\" is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is not green\" is false because the context explicitly states that Bob is green.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
