      "text": "16 says if Anne is nice and not white, then she is green. But Anne is green, so does that mean she is nice and not white? Or is it the other way around?\n\nWait, no. Point 16 is a conditional: If Anne is nice and not white, then she is green. But we know Anne is green, so the conclusion is that if she is nice and not white, then she is green. But since she is green, does that mean she is nice and not white? Not necessarily, because the converse isn't stated. So Anne could be nice and not white, but she could also be not nice or white, but in this case, she's green, so maybe she's not white.\n\nWait, but Anne's color is green, so she's not white. So if she's nice, then according to point 16, she is green, which she is. But does that mean she is nice? Not necessarily, because the rule is only one way. So Anne could be nice or not, but we don't know for sure.\n\nBut point 11 says all nice, green people are young. So if Anne is nice and green, she must be young. But we already know she's young from point 2. So that's consistent.\n\nBut wait, point 16 is a bit tricky. It says if Anne is nice and not white, then she is green. Since Anne is green, does that mean she is nice and not white? No, because the statement is only in one direction. So Anne could be nice and not white, but she could also be not nice and not white, or even nice and white, but since she's green, she's not white. So she's not white, so if she's nice, then she is green, which she is. But if she's not nice, she could still be green without being nice.\n\nBut from point 9 and 10, being white implies being nice, but Anne is green, so she's not white, so she could be nice or not. But point 13 says nice people are kind. So if Anne is nice, she's kind. But we don't have any information about Anne's kindness.\n\nWait, but point 8 says if Dave is kind and white, then he's blue. And point 14 says if Dave is blue, then he's white. Point 15 says if Dave is white and blue, then he's green. But Dave is white (point 6), so from point 9, he's nice. From point 13, nice people are kind, so Dave is kind. So from point 8, since Dave is kind and white, he must be blue. So Dave is blue (from point 8). Then from point 14, if Dave is blue, he's white, which he already is. Then from point 15, if Dave is white and blue, he's green. So Dave is green.\n\nWait, but point 4 says Charlie is green, and point 5 says Charlie is white. So Charlie is white and green. From point 11, all nice, green people are young. Charlie is green and white, so from point 9, he's nice. Therefore, Charlie must be young. But the context doesn't say that, so maybe that's an implication.\n\nBut back to Anne. She is green and young. The question is whether the statement \"Anne is young\" is true. From point 2, it's directly stated, so it's true. But maybe I need to check if any rules contradict this.\n\nWait, point 16 says if Anne is nice and not white, then she is green. Since she's green, does that mean she is nice and not white? Not necessarily, because the rule is only one way. So she could be nice and not white, but she could also be not nice and not white, but since she's green, she's not white. So she's not white, so if she's nice, she's green, which she is. But if she's not nice, she's still green, but that doesn't violate any rules.\n\nBut point 11 says all nice, green people are young. So if Anne is nice and green, she must be young. But we already know she's young, so that's consistent. But if she's not nice, then point 11 doesn't apply, so she could still be young or not. But point 2 says she is young, so regardless of her niceness, she's young.\n\nWait, but point 16 is a bit confusing. It says if Anne is nice and not white, then she is green. But she is green, so does that mean she is nice and not white? No, because the statement is only in one direction. So she could be nice and not white, but she could also be not nice and not white, but since she's green, she's not white. So she's not whi