messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat is big. The cat is nice. The cat sees the dog. The cat visits the dog. The dog is big. The dog is nice. The mouse is nice. The mouse is red. The tiger eats the cat. The tiger eats the dog. The tiger visits the mouse. If someone visits the tiger then the tiger sees the mouse. If the cat eats the dog and the cat sees the dog then the cat is big. If someone eats the dog then they visit the cat. If the tiger is young and the tiger is nice then the tiger eats the mouse. If someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red then the mouse visits the tiger. If someone visits the tiger then they visit the cat. If someone visits the cat and the cat visits the dog then the dog visits the tiger. If the cat sees the tiger then the cat eats the dog.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The mouse does not eat the mouse.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve.Let me try to break it down step by step.The context is a bunch of statements about cats, dogs, mice, and tigers, and their various actions and attributes.The question is whether the statement "The mouse does not eat the mouse" is true, false, or unknown.The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nFirst, I need to parse all the given statements carefully.Let me list them out one by one to understand the relationships and rules.\n\n1.The cat is big.\n2.The cat is nice.\n3.The cat sees the dog.\n4.The cat visits the dog.\n5.The dog is big.\n6.The dog is nice.\n7.The mouse is nice.\n8.The mouse is red.\n9.The tiger eats the cat.\n10.The tiger eats the dog.\n11.The tiger visits the mouse.\n12.If someone visits the tiger, then the tiger sees the mouse.\n13.If the cat eats the dog and the cat sees the dog, then the cat is big.\n14.If someone eats the dog, then they visit the cat.\n15.If the tiger is young and the tiger is nice, then the tiger eats the mouse.\n16.If someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger.\n17.If someone visits the tiger, then they visit the cat.\n18.If someone visits the cat and the cat visits the dog, then the dog visits the tiger.\n19.If the cat sees the tiger, then the cat eats the dog.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the mouse does not eat itself.So, I need to see if there\'s any information that suggests the mouse eats itself or not.\n\nLooking through the statements, I don\'t see any that directly mention the mouse eating anything, let alone itself.Let\'s go through each statement to see if any implications can be drawn.\n\nStatements 1-8 are basic facts about the animals.The cat is big and nice, sees and visits the dog.The dog is big and nice.The mouse is nice and red.The tiger eats the cat and the dog, visits the mouse.\n\nStatement 9: Tiger eats cat.\nStatement 10: Tiger eats dog.\nStatement 11: Tiger visits mouse.\n\nStatement 12: If someone visits the tiger, then tiger sees the mouse.So, if someone (maybe the mouse, since the tiger visits the mouse in statement 11) visits the tiger, then the tiger sees the mouse.But does the mouse visit the tiger?Statement 11 says the tiger visits the mouse, but not the other way around.So unless another statement says the mouse visits the tiger, statement 12 might not apply here.\n\nStatement 13: If cat eats dog and cat sees dog, then cat is big.Well, the cat is already big, so this might be a redundant statement, but it\'s a conditional.\n\nStatement 14: If someone eats the dog, then they visit the cat.The tiger eats the dog (statement 10), so according to this, the tiger should visit the cat.But statement 4 says the cat visits the dog, not the other way around.Wait, but statement 17 says if someone visits the tiger, they visit the cat.So if the tiger is visited by someone, that someone must visit the cat.But does the tiger get visited?Statement 11 says the tiger visits the mouse, but does the mouse visit the tiger?Statement 16 says if someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger.The mouse is red (statement 8), so if someone sees the mouse, the mouse visits the tiger.But who sees the mouse?Statement 11 says the tiger sees the mouse if someone visits the tiger (statement 12).Wait, this is getting a bit tangled.\n\nLet me try to map out the possible interactions.\n\nFrom statement 11: Tiger visits mouse.So, the tiger is at the mouse\'s location.\n\nFrom statement 12: If someone visits the tiger, then tiger sees the mouse.But the tiger is visiting the mouse, so maybe the mouse is visiting the tiger?Or perhaps someone else is visiting the tiger.But the only ones mentioned are the cat, dog, mouse, and tiger.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger.The mouse is red, so if someone sees the mouse, the mouse visits the tiger.Who sees the mouse?The tiger does, because the tiger visits the mouse (statement 11).So when the tiger visits the mouse, does that mean the tiger sees the mouse?Maybe, but statement 12 says if someone visits the tiger, then the tiger sees the mouse.So if the mouse visits the tiger (because someone saw the mouse, which is red), then the tiger sees the mouse.\n\nWait, this is a bit circular.Let me try to outline the possible chain:.\n\n1.Tiger visits mouse (statement 11).So, the tiger is at the mouse\'s location.\n\n2.When the tiger visits the mouse, does that count as someone visiting the tiger?No, because the tiger is visiting the mouse, not the other way around.So statement 12 would apply if someone else visits the tiger, causing the tiger to see the mouse.\n\n3.Alternatively, if the mouse is visited by the tiger, does that trigger any other rules?Statement 16 says if someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger.The mouse is red, so if someone sees the mouse, the mouse visits the tiger.Someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger.The mouse is red, so if someone sees the mouse, the mouse visits the tiger.But who sees the mouse?The tiger does, because the tiger visits the mouse (statement 11).So when the tiger visits the mouse, does that count as the tiger seeing the mouse?If so, then the mouse would visit the tiger.So the mouse would visit the tiger, which is statement 11 in reverse.\n\nBut does the mouse visiting the tiger lead to any action where the mouse might eat itself?I don\'t see any such rule.The only eating mentioned is the tiger eating the cat and dog, and conditionally the tiger eating the mouse if it\'s young and nice.\n\nSo, in conclusion, there\'s information about the mouse eating itself\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 13%|#3        | 79/600 [34:09<3:24:52, 23.59s/it]
