abbit sees the bald eagle.Wait, no, statement 7 is about the rabbit seeing the bald eagle.Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, the statements are:.\n\n- The rabbit sees the bald eagle.(Statement 7).\n- The rabbit is round.(Statement 9).\n- The cat likes the rabbit.(Statement 8).\n- If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round then the cat eats the rabbit.(Statement 10).\n- If someone likes the cat then the cat is round.(Statement 11).\n- If the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round then the rabbit is blue.(Statement 12).\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake earlier.Statement 7 is "The rabbit sees the bald eagle," not the cat.So, the rabbit sees the bald eagle, but does it see the cat?Let me check the context again.\n\nLooking back, the context says:.\n\n- The bald eagle sees the cat.(Statement 6).\n- The bald eagle sees the rabbit.(Statement 7).\n- The rabbit sees the bald eagle.(Statement 10).\n- The cat sees the rabbit?Wait, no, the cat is mentioned in statement 8 as liking the rabbit, but does the cat see the rabbit?Let me check.\n\nWait, the context has:.\n\n- The cat eats the bald eagle.(Statement 9).\n- The cat likes the rabbit.(Statement 8).\n- The cat sees the rabbit?Hmm, I don\'t see a statement that says the cat sees the rabbit.So, the cat likes the rabbit but doesn\'t necessarily see it.\n\nWait, but statement 10 says: If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round then the cat eats the rabbit.So, for this condition to apply, the rabbit must see the cat.But from the context, the only thing we know is that the rabbit sees the bald eagle (statement 10).There\'s no statement that says the rabbit sees the cat.So, the condition in statement 10 isn\'t met because the rabbit doesn\'t see the cat.Therefore, the conclusion that the cat eats the rabbit doesn\'t necessarily follow.\n\nWait, but the context says the cat likes the rabbit (statement 8).So, the cat likes the rabbit, but does the rabbit like the cat?That\'s the question.The statement we\'re eval