tatement \"The tiger is not big\" is false. Therefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was A) True. I'm getting confused.\n\nLet me try again.\n\nIf the tiger chases the lion, then it must be big (statement 15). So, tiger is big.\n\nIf the tiger is big, then it must visit the dog (statement 17). So, tiger visits dog.\n\nBut in the given context, the tiger visits the lion (statement 11), but not the dog. So, is there a contradiction? Or is it possible that the tiger visits both the lion and the dog?\n\nI think the tiger must visit the dog because it's big, so the fact that it's not mentioned doesn't mean it doesn't happen. Therefore, the tiger is big, making the statement \"The tiger is not big\" false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe tiger chases the lion, which according to the given rules means the tiger must be big. Therefore, the statement \"The tiger is not big\" is false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
