      "text": " A) True.\n\nWait, no, wait. Let me make sure I'm not missing anything.\n\nWait, fact 11 says \"Furry people are green.\"So, if someone is furry, they are green.Therefore, Dave, being furry, must be green.\n\nBut let me check if there's any condition that could make Dave not green.\n\nLooking at fact 14: \"All green people are big.\"So, if Dave is green, he must be big.\n\nBut does being big affect anything else? Fact 12 says \"Big, quiet people are not smart.\"But Dave's niceness is given as not nice (fact 4).Fact 13 says \"Smart people are nice.\"So, if someone is smart, they are nice. But Dave is not nice, so he can't be smart.\n\nSo, Dave is not smart.\n\nNow, if Dave is big (from fact 14, since he's green), and if he's quiet, then from fact 12, he would not be smart. But we already know he's not smart, so that's consistent.\n\nBut does being quiet affect anything else? Fact 8 says \"All round people are quiet.\"Fact 9 says \"All round people are green.\"Fact 15 says \"If Anne is big and Anne is green, then Anne is round.\"So, if Anne is big and green, she's round.\n\nBut Dave's status as round isn't directly given. However, since all round people are quiet (fact 8), if Dave were round, he'd have to be quiet. But we don't know if Dave is round or not.\n\nWait, but the main point is that Dave is furry, so he must be green (fact 11). Therefore, the statement \"Dave is green\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any possibility that being furry doesn't necessarily make someone green? No, fact 11 is a general statement: \"Furry people are green.\"So, all furry people are green, so Dave must be green.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Dave is green\" is true because all furry people are green, and Dave is furry.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
