ch that being green doesn't necessarily make you blue, but in this case, point 13 says if something is green, it is blue. So Charlie is green, so he is blue. Therefore, he is rough. But then he must be white, which contradicts his color being green.\n\nThis suggests that the statement \"Charlie is not rough\" is actually true because there's a contradiction in the premises, making it impossible for Charlie to be rough. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to outline it clearly.\n\n1. Charlie is green (point 5).\n2. If something is green, it is blue (point 13). Therefore, Charlie is blue.\n3. All blue things are rough (point 8). Therefore, Charlie is rough.\n4. Rough things are white (point 16). Therefore, Charlie is white.\n5. But Charlie is green, which is a color, so he can't be white. This is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the premises lead to a contradiction, meaning that the assumption that Charlie is green and blue and rough and white is impossible. Therefore, the statement \"Charlie is not rough\" must be true because the premises cannot all be true simultaneously, implying that Charlie cannot be rough.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rules are such that being green and blue can coexist, but the color white is separate. However, point 16 says rough things are white, so if Charlie is rough, he must be white, which contradicts his color being green. Therefore, Charlie cannot be rough, making the statement \"Charlie is not rough\" true.\n\nWait, but point 13 says if something is green, it is blue. So Charlie is green, so he is blue. Point 8 says all blue things are rough. Therefore, Charlie is rough. But point 16 says rough things are white, so Charlie must be white. But Charlie is green, which is a color, so he can't be white. Therefore, the only way to resolve this is that Charlie is not rough, making the statement true.\n\nSo, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut wait, I'm still confused because the premises lead to a contradicti