e.The lion visits the rabbit?Wait, no, the lion visits the bald eagle (statement 10) and the dog (statement 11).The rabbit visits the dog (statement 13) and the lion (statement 14).So, does something visit the rabbit?The dog visits the rabbit?Wait, statement 13 says the rabbit visits the dog, not the other way around.So, the dog is visited by the rabbit.So, does the dog visit the rabbit?Not directly stated.So, maybe the dog doesn\'t visit the rabbit, so this condition might not apply.Alternatively, maybe the lion visits the rabbit?No, the lion visits the bald eagle and the dog, not the rabbit.So, perhaps this condition isn\'t triggered.\n\nStatement 18: If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog.We know the lion needs the rabbit (statement 9), but does the dog need the rabbit?Statement 8 says the lion needs the dog, but nothing about the dog needing the rabbit.So, unless the dog needs the rabbit, this condition doesn\'t apply.Also, does the rabbit see the bald eagle?Statement 4 says the bald eagle visits the rabbit, but does that mean the rabbit sees the bald eagle?Maybe, but it\'s not explicitly stated.So, this might not be directly relevant.\n\nStatement 19: If the rabbit visits the lion and the rabbit needs the dog, then the dog is round.The rabbit does visit the lion (statement 14), and does the rabbit need the dog?Statement 8 says the lion needs the dog, but nothing about the rabbit needing the dog.So, unless the rabbit needs the dog, this condition doesn\'t apply.But since the rabbit visits the dog (statement 13), maybe that implies the rabbit needs the dog?Not necessarily, because visiting doesn\'t always mean needing.So, perhaps this condition isn\'t met.\n\nStatement 20: If something visits the rabbit, then the rabbit needs the bald eagle.The lion doesn\'t visit the rabbit, as per the given statements.The dog doesn\'t visit the rabbit either, as per the given info.The rabbit visits the dog and the lion, 