 rough), and green \u2192 blue, so Harry must be blue. So, Harry is both white and blue.\n\nNow, the question is: Is the statement \"Harry is not green\" true, false, or unknown?\n\nFrom the deductions, Harry is green because he is rough (from being big, which comes from being nice, which comes from being white). So, Harry is green. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not green\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Harry is white \u2192 nice (rule 19). Nice \u2192 big (rule 16). Big \u2192 rough (rule 13). Rough \u2192 green (rule 15). So, Harry is green. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not green\" is false, so the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything. Let me go through each step again.\n\nHarry is white (given). From rule 19, white \u2192 nice. So, Harry is nice. From rule 16, nice \u2192 big. So, Harry is big. From rule 13, big \u2192 rough. So, Harry is rough. From rule 15, rough \u2192 green. So, Harry is green. Therefore, Harry is green, so the statement \"Harry is not green\" is false.\n\nYes, that seems correct. So, the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Harry is not green\" is false because Harry is white, which makes him nice, big, rough, and therefore green.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
