messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle does not eat the mouse. The bald eagle is not rough. The bald eagle needs the lion. The bald eagle sees the lion. The lion is nice. The mouse is kind. The mouse needs the bald eagle. The mouse needs the lion. The mouse sees the lion. The rabbit eats the bald eagle. The rabbit needs the mouse. If someone eats the bald eagle and they see the bald eagle then they are big. If someone is nice and they eat the rabbit then they eat the bald eagle. If someone eats the bald eagle then they are cold. If someone needs the rabbit and they are big then the rabbit sees the mouse. If someone is cold then they need the lion. If someone is cold and big then they do not need the bald eagle. If someone needs the lion then they see the bald eagle. If the rabbit sees the bald eagle and the rabbit is not big then the bald eagle eats the lion. If the bald eagle eats the lion then the bald eagle does not see the lion.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The rabbit does not see the bald eagle.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'ve got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out whether the statement "The rabbit does not see the bald eagle" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to have a clear view:.\n\n1.The bald eagle does not eat the mouse.\n2.The bald eagle is not rough.\n3.The bald eagle needs the lion.\n4.The bald eagle sees the lion.\n5.The lion is nice.\n6.The mouse is kind.\n7.The mouse needs the bald eagle.\n8.The mouse needs the lion.\n9.The mouse sees the lion.\n10.The rabbit eats the bald eagle.\n11.The rabbit needs the mouse.\n12.If someone eats the bald eagle and they see the bald eagle, then they are big.\n13.If someone is nice and they eat the rabbit, then they eat the bald eagle.\n14.If someone eats the bald eagle, then they are cold.\n15.If someone needs the rabbit and they are big, then the rabbit sees the mouse.\n16.If someone is cold, then they need the lion.\n17.If someone is cold and big, then they do not need the bald eagle.\n18.If someone needs the lion, then they see the bald eagle.\n19.If the rabbit sees the bald eagle and the rabbit is not big, then the bald eagle eats the lion.\n20.If the bald eagle eats the lion, then the bald eagle does not see the lion.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the rabbit does not see the bald eagle.So, I need to determine if the rabbit sees the bald eagle or not.\n\nLet me start by identifying what I know about the rabbit:.\n\n- From statement 10: The rabbit eats the bald eagle.\n- From statement 11: The rabbit needs the mouse.\n\nSo, the rabbit eats the bald eagle and needs the mouse.Now, let\'s see if any of the conditional statements apply to the rabbit.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If someone eats the bald eagle and they see the bald eagle, then they are big.The rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 10), but does the rabbit see the bald eagle?That\'s what we\'re trying to find out.If the rabbit does see the bald eagle, then according to statement 12, the rabbit would be big.But if the rabbit doesn\'t see the bald eagle, then statement 12 doesn\'t apply, and we don\'t know if the rabbit is big or not.\n\nNext, statement 13: If someone is nice and they eat the rabbit, then they eat the bald eagle.The lion is nice (statement 5), so if the lion eats the rabbit, then the lion would eat the bald eagle.But does the lion eat the rabbit?I don\'t see any statement that says the lion eats the rabbit.The only eater mentioned is the rabbit eating the bald eagle.So, unless there\'s more info, I can\'t assume the lion eats the rabbit.\n\nStatement 14: If someone eats the bald eagle, then they are cold.The rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 10), so according to statement 14, the rabbit is cold.\n\nStatement 15: If someone needs the rabbit and they are big, then the rabbit sees the mouse.The mouse needs the rabbit (statement 11), so the mouse needs the rabbit.If the mouse is big, then the rabbit sees the mouse.But I don\'t know if the mouse is big.The mouse is kind (statement 6), but that doesn\'t necessarily mean it\'s big.So, unless the mouse is big, this doesn\'t help us directly.\n\nStatement 16: If someone is cold, then they need the lion.The rabbit is cold (from statement 14), so according to statement 16, the rabbit needs the lion.The rabbit is not big, so the bald eagle does not eat the lion.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 44%|####3     | 263/600 [1:37:32<35:14,  6.27s/it]
