y eats the cow (statement 1), so the lion doesn't eat the cow. Therefore, the condition is false, so the conclusion is irrelevant. So, the lion doesn't eat the cow, so the lion doesn't visit the bald eagle because the condition is false.\n\nWait, no, in logic, if the condition is false, the implication is true regardless of the conclusion. So, the statement doesn't give us any new information about the lion's actions beyond that.\n\nSo, I think I've checked all the statements, and none of them mention the lion's shape. Therefore, the statement \"The lion is round\" is unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe lion's shape is not mentioned in the context, so the statement is unknown.\n\nC) Unknown",
