e, then it visits the bear.\n15.If something likes the lion and it is blue, then the lion is nice.\n16.If something eats the mouse, then it likes the lion.\n17.If the bear is not green, then the bear likes the lion.\n18.If something visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion.\n19.If something likes the lion and the lion is blue, then the lion likes the mouse.\n20.If something is nice, then it is blue.\n\nThe question is whether the squirrel is cold.From statement 10, it says "The squirrel is not cold."So that directly answers the question.But wait, maybe I should check if there are any other statements that could affect this conclusion.\n\nLet me go through each statement to see if any of them might influence the squirrel\'s temperature.\n\nStarting with statement 10: "The squirrel is not cold."That\'s straightforward.So according to this, the squirrel is not cold, meaning the statement "The squirrel is cold" would be false.\n\nBut let me make sure there\'s no contradiction elsewhere.Let\'s look at other statements that might relate to the squirrel.\n\nStatement 5: The lion visits the squirrel.So the lion visits the squirrel, but I don\'t see how that affects the squirrel\'s temperature.\n\nStatement 6: The mouse eats the squirrel.So the mouse is eating the squirrel.Then, according to statement 12, if something eats the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the mouse.So since the mouse eats the squirrel, the squirrel must eat the mouse.But that\'s a bit of a paradox because the mouse is eating the squirrel, and the squirrel is eating the mouse.That seems like a cycle, but maybe it\'s just a hypothetical situation.\n\nBut does this affect whether the squirrel is cold?I don\'t think so.It\'s more about their interactions.\n\nStatement 9: The mouse visits the squirrel.So the mouse and squirrel interact, but again, not directly related to temperature.\n\nStatement 11: The squirrel is nice.So the squirrel is nice.Then, statement 20 says if something is nice, then it is blue.So the squirrel is nice, therefore the squirrel is blue.\n\nNow, if the squirrel is blue, does that affect anything else?Let\'s see.\nI see many contradictions in the statements.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 54%|#####4    | 326/600 [1:54:09<18:36,  4.08s/it]
