      "text": "16. All white things are green.\n\nWait, but Erin is young, so she's white (from fact 13), and since white things are green (fact 16), Erin is green.But fact 11 says if Erin is green, then she is white, which is already the case, so that's consistent.\n\nNow, looking at Harry: he's white (fact 7) and kind (fact 6).From fact 14: If Harry is white, then he is young.So Harry is white, so he must be young.\n\nSo Harry is young, white, and kind.\n\nNow, let's go back to Charlie.Charlie is blue (fact 2), not green (fact 3), nice (fact 4), and kind (from fact 10 because he's nice).\n\nNow, the question is whether Charlie is not young.So is Charlie young or not?\n\nFrom the facts, we know that if something is young, it's white (fact 13).But Charlie is blue, not green (fact 3).Wait, but from fact 12: All green things are blue, but Charlie is not green, so he's blue, which is consistent.\n\nBut does being blue imply anything about being young?Let's see.\n\nIf something is young, it's white (fact 13).But Charlie is blue, not white, so can he be young?\n\nWait, if Charlie were young, he would have to be white (fact 13).But Charlie is blue, not white, so he can't be young because that would require him to be white, which contradicts his color.\n\nTherefore, Charlie cannot be young, so the statement \"Charlie is not young\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nCharlie is blue, not green, nice, and kind.\n\nIf Charlie were young, he would have to be white (fact 13).But Charlie is blue, not white, so he can't be young.\n\nTherefore, Charlie is not young, so the statement is true.\n\nSo the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Charlie is not young\" is true because if Charlie were young, he would have to be white, but he is blue, which contradicts that. \n\n**Answer: A) True**",
