      "text": "17. If something is smart and not young, then it is blue.\n\nWait, but Bob is blue, so that fits. But does Bob being smart and not young make him blue? Yes, but we need to see if Bob is young or not.\n\nFrom point 13: If something is furry, then it is young. Harry is furry, so Harry is young. Harry is also nice, so from point 12, young, nice things are furry, which Harry is.\n\nNow, looking at Bob: He is round, so from point 15, he's smart. From point 11, all smart things are furry. So Bob must be furry. But from point 13, if something is furry, it's young. So Bob is young.\n\nFrom point 16: If Bob is young, then he is nice. Since Bob is young, he must be nice. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is not nice\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Bob is round, so smart. Smart things are furry, so Bob is furry. Furry implies young. So Bob is young. Then, from point 16, if Bob is young, he's nice. So Bob is nice. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is not nice\" is false.\n\nI think that's correct. So the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Bob is not nice\" is false because Bob is young (as he is furry) and thus must be nice.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
