se.\n\nBut I'm not sure if that's the correct approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel is not blue because it's green, and the rules don't allow it to be both.\n\nBut the rules don't say that, so I think the correct approach is that the squirrel is blue because it sees the tiger, despite being green.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is false.\n\nBut I'm not entirely confident.\n\nWait, let me try to outline the logical steps:\n\n1. Squirrel is green (7).\n\n2. Squirrel chases lion (5).\n\n3. Lion eats squirrel (2).\n\n4. Therefore, from statement 13, squirrel sees tiger.\n\n5. From statement 15, if squirrel sees tiger, it is blue.\n\n6. Therefore, squirrel is blue.\n\n7. But squirrel is also green.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel is both green and blue.\n\nBut the question is whether the squirrel is not blue.\n\nSince the squirrel is blue, the statement is false.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut I'm not sure if the squirrel can be both green and blue.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel is not blue because it's green, and the rules don't allow it to be both.\n\nBut the rules don't specify that, so I think the correct approach is that the squirrel is blue because it sees the tiger, despite being green.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but the squirrel is green, so it can't be blue.\n\nBut according to the rules, if it sees the tiger, it must be blue.\n\nSo, the squirrel is both green and blue.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not blue\" is false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut I'm still a bit confused because in reality, something can't be both green and blue, but in logic puzzles, sometimes such things are allowed unless specified otherwise.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe squirrel is blue because it sees the tiger, as per the given rules, despite being green. Therefore, the statement \"The squ