kes it? I'm not sure.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me try to map out the relationships.\n\nKnown facts:\n\n- Cow sees squirrel (statement 6)\n- Lion sees squirrel (statement 11)\n- Lion needs squirrel (statement 10)\n- Lion is kind and nice (statements 7,8)\n- Lion likes bald eagle (statement 9)\n- Squirrel likes lion (statement 12)\n- Bald eagle needs cow and squirrel (statements 2,3)\n- Cow is blue (statement 4)\n- Cow likes bald eagle (statement 5)\n- If something sees lion, lion likes squirrel (statement 13)\n- If something is kind and sees bald eagle, it needs cow (statement 14)\n- If something sees squirrel and bald eagle, bald eagle likes cow (statement 15)\n- If something sees lion and lion likes squirrel, lion needs squirrel (statement 16)\n- If something likes squirrel and needs squirrel, squirrel sees cow (statement 17)\n- If something sees cow, it sees lion (statement 18)\n- If something needs cow and squirrel, it likes squirrel (statement 19)\n- If something sees lion, lion sees bald eagle (statement 20)\n- If something sees cow and likes cow, cow sees bald eagle (statement 21)\n\nNow, let's see if we can derive any new information.\n\nFrom statement 18: If something sees the cow, then it sees the lion.\n\nSo, if the squirrel sees the cow, then the squirrel sees the lion.\n\nIf the squirrel sees the lion, then from statement 20: The lion sees the bald eagle.\n\nSo, if the squirrel sees the cow, then the lion sees the bald eagle.\n\nBut does the lion see the bald eagle? Let's see.\n\nFrom statement 9: The lion likes the bald eagle. Does that mean the lion sees the bald eagle? Not necessarily.\n\nBut if the squirrel sees the cow, then the lion sees the bald eagle.\n\nNow, from statement 15: If something sees the squirrel and the bald eagle, then the bald eagle likes the cow.\n\nSo, if the lion sees the squirrel (which it does, statement 11) and the lion sees the bald eagle (which would happen if the squirrel sees the cow), then the bald eagle likes