      "index": 0,
      "text": " Dave is big, white, round.\n\nNow, the question is: Is Dave young?\n\nFrom the context, Dave is big, so he's white and round.\n\nFrom rule 15, all round things are green.So Dave is green.\n\nFrom rule 20, all green things are red.So Dave is red.\n\nFrom rule 14, all white, red things are rough.So Dave is white and red, so he must be rough.\n\nSo Dave is big, white, round, green, red, and rough.\n\nNow, does Dave being rough and young imply anything? From rule 18, all rough, young things are red.Dave is rough, but we don't know if he's young.\n\nWait, but from rule 19, if something is young, it is green.Dave is green, but that doesn't necessarily mean he's young because rule 19 is one-way: young implies green, but green doesn't necessarily imply young.\n\nWait, but rule 19 says if something is young, then it is green. It doesn't say that if something is green, it is young. So Dave being green doesn't necessarily mean he's young.\n\nBut let's see if there's any rule that connects being round or big to being young.\n\nFrom rule 17, if Harry is round, then he is big. But that's specific to Harry.\n\nFrom rule 13, all big things are round, but that doesn't say anything about being young.\n\nFrom rule 12, all rough, white things are young. Dave is rough and white, so according to rule 12, he must be young.\n\nWait, that's a key point. Rule 12 says all rough, white things are young. Dave is rough and white, so he must be young.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought Dave is big, white, round, green, red, and rough. So he is rough and white, so according to rule 12, he must be young.\n\nBut the context says Dave is big, but doesn't mention him being young. So according to the rules, Dave must be young.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nRule 12: All rough, white things are young.\n\nDave is rough (from rule 14, since he's white and red, so he's rough) and white (from rule 16, being big). So Dave is rough and white, so he must be young.\n\nTherefore, Dave is young.\n\nWait, but the context doesn't explicitly say Dave is young, but according to the rules, he must be.\n\nSo the statement \"Dave is young\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nAnother way: Let's see if there's any contradiction.\n\nIf Dave is big, he's white and round (rules 16 and 13). Being round, he's green (rule 15). Being green, he's red (rule 20). Being white and red, he's rough (rule 14). Being rough and white, he's young (rule 12). So yes, Dave must be young.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Dave is young\" is true based on the given rules. \n\n**Answer: A) True**",
