ry is not blue, so the condition (Harry being blue) is false.In logic, if the condition is false, the implication is automatically true, so this doesn\'t give us new information about Harry\'s niceness or roundness.\n\nLooking at statement 9: If something is young and round, then it\'s rough.Harry is young, so if he were round, he\'d be rough.But we don\'t know yet if he\'s round.\n\nStatement 10: All young, nice things are rough.So if Harry is nice, then he must be rough.But we don\'t know if Harry is nice yet.\n\nStatement 13: Nice things are quiet.So if Harry is nice, he\'s quiet.\n\nStatement 14: All quiet things are round.So if Harry is quiet, he\'s round.\n\nPutting this together: If Harry is nice, he\'s quiet (from 13), which would make him round (from 14).Then, from statement 9, being young and round would make him rough.But from statement 15: Rough things are not red.However, we know Harry is red (statement 6).So if Harry were rough, he couldn\'t be red, which contradicts statement 6.Harry is not round, which means he must be nice, young, and red, according to the statements provided.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
