      "text": " B) False.\n\nWait, no, because if the squirrel is cold, then the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false, so the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure I didn't miss anything.\n\nAnother angle: From point 10, since the mouse is cold and sees the squirrel, the squirrel is cold.\n\nSo, squirrel is cold.Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check if the squirrel being cold affects other statements.\n\nIf the squirrel is cold, then from point 8, it should see the rabbit.Does the squirrel see the rabbit?From point 6, the rabbit sees the squirrel, but does the squirrel see the rabbit?Not necessarily, unless the squirrel is cold and thus sees the rabbit.\n\nWait, point 8 says if something is cold, it sees the rabbit.So, if the squirrel is cold, then it sees the rabbit.\n\nSo, squirrel sees rabbit.\n\nBut from point 13: If the rabbit sees the squirrel (which it does) and the squirrel chases the rabbit, then the rabbit does not chase the squirrel.But we concluded earlier that the squirrel does not chase the rabbit, so point 13 doesn't apply.\n\nWait, but if the squirrel is cold and sees the rabbit, then from point 9, it should see the squirrel, but the squirrel is already seeing the rabbit, so that's consistent.\n\nI think the key point is that the mouse is cold, sees the squirrel, so the squirrel is cold.Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.\n\nSo, the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe squirrel is cold because the mouse, being cold, sees the squirrel, which according to the given rules, makes the squirrel cold. Therefore, the statement \"The squirrel is not cold\" is false.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
